{#- Listen2 2.0 draft: the Listen2 Voices block, first inside the Voices section. One sample slot per voice from
    src/_data/listen2Voices.json. Claims 2, 3, 4, 6: plan section 5. Accent labels (Zach, 2026-11-23): each voice
    is labelled American or British, or another accent such as Scottish where confirmed, from the roster's
    "accent" field. Zach is labelling them by ear: leave "accent" null until he does; never guess. -#}
{% from "components/listen2-2/slots.njk" import audioSlot %}
        <div id="listen2-voices" style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px">
          <div style="display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; gap: 6px 14px">
            <h3 style="margin: 0; font-size: 22px; font-weight: 700"><span style="display: inline-block; width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 5px; background: #FF66C4; margin-right: 10px; vertical-align: middle"></span>Listen2 Voices</h3>
            <span style="font-size: 16px; color: #A3A3A0">{{ listen2Voices.voiceCount }} English voices from our own engine, new in 2.0</span>
          </div>
          <p style="margin: 0; max-width: 820px; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.6; color: #D4D4D0">We trained this engine ourselves, from scratch, on 7,410 hours of speech from 7,924 speakers. It runs entirely on your iPhone, with no account and no network needed to speak, so no audio or text leaves your phone. In our tests it read about 99 of every 100 words exactly as written, on old books and on modern text alike, and in nine hours of test reading it did not skip a clause.</p>
          <p style="margin: 0; max-width: 820px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.55; color: #A3A3A0">Every voice below reads the same sentence, the sample you hear in the app, so you can compare the voices and not the texts. Twenty-six are American, six British and two Australian. Graham reads to you first.</p>
          <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 16px 20px">
            {%- for v in listen2Voices.roster %}
            {{ audioSlot(v.name + (v.accent and (" <span class=\"l2-slot-tag\">" + v.accent + "</span>") or ""), listen2Voices.voiceDir + "listen2-" + v.id + ".mp3", v.ready) }}
            {%- endfor %}
          </div>
          <details class="l2-measured" style="max-width: 860px; border-top: 1px solid #2A2A2D; border-bottom: 1px solid #2A2A2D; padding: 8px 0">
            <summary>How we measured this</summary>
            <p style="margin: 8px 0 0">The figures on this page come from our own test: 6,027 clips, about nine hours of speech, with the same texts and voices every time. Two speech recognizers wrote down what each clip said, and we compared that with the text. No panel of listeners was involved.</p>
            <ul style="margin: 12px 0 0; padding-left: 20px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px">
              <li>Words read exactly as written: about 99 in 100, on old-book text and on modern text. The recognizers make mistakes of their own, so the true rate is a little better than that.</li>
              <li>Hard modern words (brands, technical terms, recent names): 98.8 in 100 heard correctly. The list is our own, tested in 724 sentences written around those words, and it is not an industry test.</li>
              <li>Numbers and initialisms: fewer than 1 character in 100 wrong. This depends on how Listen2 prepares the text as much as on the voice.</li>
              <li>Skipped text: none. In 192 long paragraphs, no run of four or more words went missing.</li>
              <li>Naturalness: an automatic score that predicts how listeners would rate a clip put the voices in the range of real human recordings. That is a machine's estimate, not a listening study.</li>
            </ul>
          </details>
        </div>
